  message: "FOLDS IN WI-jufub (owner 09-29). Same root: WI-tolif/WI-dutah's stdio -> logging ruling never reached two catalogues. Same fix, same test pattern (test_python_catalog_stdio_is_logging_not_ipc_send), same A/B with every removed finding read back.\n\nSCOPE NOW, swept across every io_primitives catalogue through load_catalog on dev 596b845156: the ONLY stdout/stderr rows still under ipc_send are\n  go:   os.Stdout, os.Stderr            (attribute, ipc_send)   <- from WI-jufub\n  java: java.lang.System.out, .err      (attribute, ipc_send)   kotlin + scala inherit\nstdin stays ipc_recv in EVERY catalogue (c, cpp, go, java, javascript, python, rust, kotlin, scala): consistent, and right (piped-in data is attacker-chosen), so it is NOT in scope.\n\nWI-jufub's MEASURED REPRO (dev 45fc5a483a), carried here: a Go file whose one statement is io.WriteString(os.Stderr, os.Getenv(\"API_KEY\")) gives io-boundaries ipc_send os.Stderr beside logging io.WriteString, and verify-claims host_secret -> ipc VIOLATED through the attribute reference (sink_primitives [os.Stderr]). One write, two zones. Any mention of os.Stderr (log.SetOutput(os.Stderr)) is an ipc sink. go.yaml's own io.WriteString / fmt.Fprint* / os.File notes already cite WI-dutah.\n\nStatus raised to todo_hard: this now carries a measured false violation.\nNeighbours, NOT folded: WI-dorus (java receiver typing on System.out calls), INV-hopib (a sink row naming a stream object matches a non-call use; persists under logging)."  # e364
